    "title": "Snowflake AI Data Cloud Features & Architecture",
    "body": "The heaviest domain on the exam and the highest-ROI study area. C03 pushed this from 25% to ~31% because Iceberg, Cortex, Snowpark, and Streamlit are all new architectural primitives.\n\n### 1.2 Three-layer architecture\n\nBe able to explain all three layers in one breath:\n\n| Layer | What it does | Key facts |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Database Storage | Compressed, columnar, immutable micro-partitions on S3 / Azure Blob / GCS | You never touch the files; Snowflake manages everything |\n| Compute (Virtual Warehouses) | MPP clusters that execute queries | T-shirt sizes XS through 6XL; auto-suspend / auto-resume; scale up or out |\n| Cloud Services | The brain: auth, metadata, query parsing & optimization, access control | Stateless, fully managed; you never provision it |\n\n### 1.4 Editions\n\n| Edition | Distinguishing features |\n| --- | --- |\n| Standard | 1-day Time Travel |\n| Enterprise | Up to 90-day Time Travel, Materialized Views, Multi-Cluster Warehouses, Search Optimization, masking policies |\n| Business Critical | HIPAA/PCI support, Tri-Secret Secure, PrivateLink, failover groups |\n| VPS | Dedicated, isolated environment for the most regulated buyers |\n\n> **Trap: \"Which feature requires Enterprise?\" questions are frequent. Memorize the Enterprise list above cold.**\n\n### 1.5 Table and view types\n\n| Type | Time Travel | Fail-safe | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Permanent | 0–90 days | 7 days | Default |\n| Transient | 0–1 day | None | Lower storage cost |\n| Temporary | 0–1 day | None | Session-scoped; dropped at session end |\n| External | N/A | N/A | Read-only pointer to files in object storage |\n| Iceberg New · C03 | Varies | N/A | Open table format; Snowflake-managed or external catalog |\n| Dynamic New · C03 | — | — | Declarative target state refreshed on a TARGET_LAG |\n| Hybrid (Unistore) | — | — | Row-store for transactional workloads |\n\nViews: standard, secure (hides definition, required for sharing), and materialized (Enterprise+, auto-maintained, storage + credit cost).\n\n### 1.3 Interfaces & connectivity\n\nSnowsight (web UI), SnowSQL (legacy CLI), the newer Snowflake CLI, drivers (JDBC, ODBC, Python, .NET, Node.js, Go), and connectors (Kafka, Spark). Git integrationNew · C03 connects GitHub / GitLab / Bitbucket / Azure DevOps repos to Snowflake as database objects; Workspaces can fetch and execute files from the repo.\n\n### 1.6 AI / ML & app development New · C03\n\n| Service | Input | Output / use |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Cortex AI SQL functions | String column | COMPLETE, SENTIMENT, TRANSLATE, SUMMARIZE, EMBED_TEXT — in-SQL NLP |\n| Cortex Search | Unstructured docs | Relevance-ranked chunks — RAG |\n| Cortex Analyst | NL question + semantic model | Text-to-SQL over structured tables |\n| Document AI | PDFs / images | Structured JSON extraction |\n\n> **Trap: Search vs Analyst. Unstructured document retrieval = Cortex Search. Natural-language questions over structured tables = Cortex Analyst. The exam swaps them deliberately.**\n\n```sql\n-- Cortex in plain SQL (C03 territory)\r\nSELECT ticket_id,\r\n       SNOWFLAKE.CORTEX.SENTIMENT(msg)  AS sentiment,\r\n       SNOWFLAKE.CORTEX.SUMMARIZE(msg)  AS summary\r\nFROM support_tickets\r\nWHERE created_at > CURRENT_DATE - 7;\n```\n\nAlso know: Snowflake Notebooks (native Jupyter-like), Streamlit in Snowflake, Snowpark (Python/Java/Scala pushdown), Snowflake ML (registry, feature store).\n\n### Topic checklist",
