    "title": "Performance Optimization, Querying & Transformation",
    "body": "### 4.1 Micro-partitions & pruning\n\nEvery table is automatically split into 50–500 MB (uncompressed) compressed, columnar, immutable micro-partitions. Snowflake keeps min/max metadata per column per partition, enabling pruning: skipping partitions that cannot contain the filtered value. Updates write new partitions and mark old ones deleted. Inspect with SYSTEM$CLUSTERING_INFORMATION('t','(col)').\n\nClustering keys: only for large tables (roughly 1 TB+) with selective predicates. Automatic Clustering costs credits and runs asynchronously; monitor via AUTOMATIC_CLUSTERING_HISTORY. Clustering is not an index — one clustering key per table, which can use expressions like DATE_TRUNC.\n\n### 4.1 The three caches\n\n| Cache | Where | Lifetime | Holds |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Result cache | Cloud Services (account-wide) | 24h, extended on reuse up to 31 days | Exact query results |\n| Metadata cache | Cloud Services | Persistent | Stats: COUNT, MIN, MAX — answered without a warehouse |\n| Warehouse (data) cache | Local SSD per warehouse | Until suspend | Micro-partition data |\n\n> **Trap: auto-suspend saves credits but drops the warehouse data cache. Aggressive suspend + repetitive queries = cold reads. The exam tests this tension.**\n\n### 4.2 Warehouse sizing decisions\n\nScale UP (bigger size) for a single heavy query, spilling, big joins. Scale OUT (Multi-Cluster, Enterprise+) for concurrency and queuing. Separate warehouses per workload. Query Profile red flags: bytes spilled to local/remote storage (too small), poor pruning (clustering problem), exploding joins (predicates), queuing (saturation). Query Acceleration ServiceNew · C03 offloads heavy scans to shared compute.\n\n### 4.3 SQL extensions & procedural code\n\nQUALIFY (filter window functions), PIVOT / UNPIVOT, MERGE, multi-table INSERT, CREATE TABLE ... CLONE. UDFs in SQL, JavaScript, Python, Java; stored procedures in SQL, JavaScript, Python, Scala. Snowpark DataFrames push computation into the warehouse — data never leaves Snowflake.\n\n### 4.4 Semi-structured & unstructured\n\n```sql\n-- FLATTEN nested JSON\r\nSELECT f.value:sku::STRING AS sku\r\nFROM raw_orders r,\r\nLATERAL FLATTEN(input => r.payload:items) f;\n```\n\nVARIANT, OBJECT, ARRAY types; PARSE_JSON, colon-path navigation, :: casting, LATERAL FLATTEN. Unstructured: directory tables over stages, Document AI for extraction.\n\n### Topic checklist",
