    "title": "How to use this guide — what the evidence says",
    "body": "Passive rereading and highlighting rank near the bottom of ten study techniques evaluated in Dunlosky et al.'s 2013 review (Psychological Science in the Public Interest). The techniques below are what the same literature ranks highest. Effortful beats comfortable, every time it has been measured.\n\n### 01 · HIGHEST LEVERAGE — Retrieval practice\n\nClose the material and force recall. Testing yourself beats rereading by a wide margin. Every checklist item here is a retrieval prompt: don't tick it until you can explain it with the guide closed.\n\n*Roediger & Karpicke, 2006, Psychological Science*\n\n### 02 — Spaced repetition\n\nSpread exposures over days and weeks instead of massing them. Spacing reliably beats cramming for retention — the reason the plan below revisits every domain across multiple weeks.\n\n*Cepeda et al., 2006, Psychological Bulletin (meta-analysis)*\n\n### 03 — Interleaving\n\nMix problem types instead of blocking one topic at a time. Feels worse, tests better — and mirrors the real exam, which shuffles all five domains together.\n\n*Rohrer & Taylor, 2007, Instructional Science*\n\n### 04 — Elaboration\n\nExplain the material in your own words and ask why it works. Why does a transient table skip Fail-safe? Why is a direct share same-region only? The \"why\" is what survives to exam day.\n\n*Chi et al., 1994, Cognitive Science (self-explanation effect)*\n\nThe protocol: read one section of the Morton book (or a domain below) once. Close it. Write or say everything you remember. Check the gaps against this guide. Revisit that same section 2–3 days later. Weight full practice exams heavily — they are retrieval practice in its purest form. Only tick a checklist item after a successful closed-book recall, not after a comfortable reread.",
